remediation:
Implement strict HTTP method validation in the logout endpoint to only allow GET or POST methods. Add the following code at the beginning of /logout.php before any other processing:

```php
if (!in_array($_SERVER['REQUEST_METHOD'], ['GET', 'POST'])) {
    header('HTTP/1.1 405 Method Not Allowed');
    header('Allow: GET, POST');
    http_response_code(405);
    exit('Method Not Allowed');
}
```

Alternatively, implement a centralized request router or middleware that enforces method restrictions application-wide. Additionally, convert logout to POST-only to prevent CSRF via GET requests:

```php
if ($_SERVER['REQUEST_METHOD'] !== 'POST') {
    header('HTTP/1.1 405 Method Not Allowed');
    header('Allow: POST');
    http_response_code(405);
    exit('Method Not Allowed');
}
```

Ensure CSRF tokens are validated on the POST request as well.
